1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:02,364
What if India never gave the world zero?

2
00:00:02,504 --> 00:00:03,974
Zero is just a circle.

3
00:00:04,030 --> 00:00:06,055
But try writing one thousand without it.

4
00:00:06,055 --> 00:00:07,705
Around fourteen hundred years ago,

5
00:00:07,895 --> 00:00:12,825
Indian mathematicians like Brahmagupta
wrote the rules for zero as a real number.

6
00:00:12,851 --> 00:00:15,801
Other cultures used a gap
or a dot as a placeholder.

7
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,662
India turned it into something you
can add, subtract and calculate with.

8
00:00:19,662 --> 00:00:22,832
Without it, we might be stuck
with systems like Roman numerals.

9
00:00:22,847 --> 00:00:25,737
Try multiplying XLVII by CXXIII.

10
00:00:26,087 --> 00:00:29,457
Algebra, modern mathematics
and computers all lean on zero.

11
00:00:29,538 --> 00:00:31,008
Every phone, every app,

12
00:00:31,418 --> 00:00:33,788
every UPI payment is just ones and zeros.

13
00:00:33,908 --> 00:00:35,658
Someone else might have found it later.

14
00:00:35,774 --> 00:00:36,744
Maybe much later.

15
00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:38,414
Historians can only guess.

16
00:00:38,549 --> 00:00:40,239
Nothing, it turns out, was everything.

17
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:42,420
So what if India never invented zero?

